💡 **SUGGESTION: `handlePreMergeGate` treats any unknown readiness as `conflict`**

The pre-refactor code whitelisted the escalation (`readiness === 'conflict'` escalated; any unrecognized value fell through to the merge attempt). The extraction inverts that default: everything that is not `retry`/`updateBranch` now escalates the ticket to In Rework:

```javascript
function handlePreMergeGate(gate) {
    if (gate.readiness === 'retry') { /* wait */ }
    if (gate.readiness === 'updateBranch') { /* request update, wait */ }
    console.log('PR has merge conflict — moving ticket to In Rework');   // ← catch-all fall-through
    escalateToInRework({ ... });
```

Today this is behavior-identical: `decideMergeReadiness` (js/retryMergePR.js:153) returns a closed set — `retry | updateBranch | conflict | merge` — and the gate is only entered when `readiness !== 'merge'`, so exactly `conflict` reaches the fall-through. I verified this branch-for-branch.

But it is a latent trap: a future readiness value added to `decideMergeReadiness` without updating this helper would silently escalate live tickets to In Rework instead of being a no-op (the old shape's failure mode was the milder "attempt the merge"). Consider making the escalation branch explicit, e.g.:

```javascript
if (gate.readiness !== 'conflict') {
    throw new Error('unhandled merge readiness: ' + gate.readiness);
}
console.log('PR has merge conflict — moving ticket to In Rework');
```

so an unmapped value fails loudly in the log instead of moving tickets.

💡 **SUGGESTION: the hoisted `noteRedSlotHolder` doc lost its attribution and the live-incident citation**

The original in-`action()` comment carried the provenance this repo deliberately keeps for archaeology:

```javascript
// Owner directive 2026-10-04 ("red yields the slot") — diagnostic only:
// once per run, when the first approved PR is deferred as FIFO-queued,
// probe whether the ai_validating holder's validation has CONCLUDED RED
// (an in-flight run reads 'pending' and stays silent). A red holder
// means the SM's fail-validation should have freed the slot — the line
// makes the starvation visible in the bot log (live fa 11:0x: #1194
// held a concluded-red arm while #1215..#1223 queued 40+ min). The bot
// itself NEVER acts on it (no unarm, no dispatch — SM-owned).
```

The hoisted doc block re-labels the helper "gh-766 dead-head predicate support (gh-1185 hoist)" and keeps only a summary. Three things are gone:

1. the **owner-directive attribution** (2026-10-04, "red yields the slot") — the actual origin of this diagnostic, which is not gh-766;
2. the **in-flight silence detail** — an armed-but-pending holder reads `'pending'` and stays silent, which is why the probe only speaks on CONCLUDED RED;
3. the **live-fa starvation incident** (#1194 held a concluded-red arm while #1215..#1223 queued 40+ min) — the exact failure the line exists to make visible.

The code move itself is verbatim (confirmed against the base). Please carry the citation along with the code, e.g. append to the doc block:

```
 * Owner directive 2026-10-04 ("red yields the slot"); live fa 11:0x:
 * #1194 held a concluded-red arm while #1215..#1223 queued 40+ min.
 * An in-flight run reads 'pending' and stays silent — only a CONCLUDED
 * RED holder speaks.
```

